<header><p class="st-kicker">imperial city · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free Imperial City of Landau</h1><p class="st-meta">1291 CE – 1680 CE (approx.) · capital Landau</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Palatinate wine town that held imperial-city status for four centuries before Louis XIV annexed it and made it a fortress.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Founded by the counts of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-leiningen">Leiningen</a> around 1260, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landau">Landau</a> received town rights from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-i-of-germany">King Rudolf I</a> in 1274, and in 1291 King Rudolf I granted it the privileges of a free imperial city. In the 1320s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-iv-holy-roman-emperor">Louis the Bavarian</a> pledged the town to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-speyer">Speyer</a>, and Landau had to buy back its freedom, which it achieved only in 1511.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A walled market town in the vineyards of the southern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Palatinate</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landau">Landau</a> was governed by a council and lived from wine, crafts and trade. From 1521 it belonged to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/decapolis">Decapolis</a>, the league of Alsatian imperial cities, and it accepted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in the 16th century. Its position made it a target in every war along the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> in 1648 placed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/decapolis">Decapolis</a> under French protection, and in 1680 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reunions">Chambers of Reunion</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landau">Landau</a> outright; Vauban rebuilt it as a great fortress. It passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria</a> in 1816 and is now Landau in der Pfalz in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/free-imperial-city-of-landau.jpg" alt="Free Imperial City of Landau" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: BertholdD · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Court_House_in_Landau.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1291 CE – 1680 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>imperial city</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Landau</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Landau in der Pfalz</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1486&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-landau" aria-label="Free Imperial City of Landau on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4867 462 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Free Imperial City of Landau lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5187 718L5918 1245M5187 718L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5918 1245h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5187 718h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5187 718h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1486&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-landau">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

